## Descriptor and opcode table

`opcode-table` is derived programmatically, not hand-copied: `carried-
mnemonics` = `(disj code/mnemonics :var :closure)` (18 of the 21 named
mnemonics), and each carried mnemonic's `[attribute kind]` slots are read
straight off `code/vector-operand-table` and passed through `renamespace-
slots`, which only rewrites the attribute's namespace to `:yin.debruijn.
code/*`, keeping kind and order exactly as the named table states them. A
future change to `vector-operand-table`'s shape for a carried mnemonic is
inherited automatically. Only `:load-bound` ([depth :uint] [position
:uint]), `:load-free` ([name :sym]), and `:closure` ([arity :uint] [body
:pc]) are hand-declared, because their shapes are new by design, not
carried. `opcode-table-derives-from-the-named-table` asserts, for every
carried mnemonic, equal operand count, equal kind sequence, and that every
attribute is renamespaced; it also asserts the three redefined mnemonics'
exact shapes and that `:var` is gone.

`mnemonics` = `(set (keys opcode-table))`, 22 entries (18 carried + load-
bound + load-free + closure).

`descriptor` is a small d3-triple vector, the same shape `yin.vm.debruijn/
descriptor` uses for its own dimension: `:dim/lowering-contract-version`
(1), `:dim/arity` (`(count opcode-table)`, 22), `:dim/slots` (the opcode
table itself), `:dim/encoding` (a map naming the hash function, the H
formula in prose, the scalar-class list, the framing rule, the two fixed-
width kinds, and explicit `:nfc false` / `:integral-double-folding false`
flags), and `:dim/lift-to` -- one entry naming `:yin.code/*` as the lift
target and stating the rule in prose ("diagnostic binder names or
synthesized names"), with no implementation: B1 declares this as a
documented contract point only, per the file box; B2 owns the function.
`descriptor-declares-the-contract` checks the version, the arity, the
presence of the lift-to entry, and its target keyword.

## Scalar encoder and what it copied from the projection

Copied verbatim from `yin.vm.debruijn` (that namespace documents these as
meant to be copied, not imported): `to-hex`, `code-unit-at`, `utf8-byte-
length`, the `framed` shape (tag + 8-hex UTF-8 length + content, rebuilt
here over this file's own `class-tag` table rather than the projection's),
`int64-le-hex`, `double-le-hex`. None of `yin.vm.debruijn`'s private vars
are referenced; `code/terminators` (public) is reused directly for the
generic terminator rule, since :jump/:return/:halt are unchanged mnemonics.

`well-formed-utf16?` and `ident-parts-ok?` are independently rewritten to
the same rule (unpaired UTF-16 surrogates refused), not copied, per this
phase's explicit instruction ("match the rule, not the code").

`scalar-classes` = `[:nil :bool :long :double :ratio :bigint :char :string
:keyword :symbol :vector :list :map :set]` (14 classes; tag = table
index). `scalar-class` recursively classifies a value, with no
normalization step anywhere: strings and identifier parts are checked for
well-formed UTF-16 only, never NFC'd; integral doubles are never folded
into :long. `encode-scalar` throws `{:rule :unsupported-value, :value v}`
for anything `scalar-class` returns nil for (including a map/set whose
canonical encoding collides -- reused from the projection's own collision-
detection shape, independently written). Maps and sets sort their encoded
entries as text (`sort` over the encoded hex strings), satisfying "ordered
by encoded bytes, not host comparison."

## Host-specific decisions

- **1 vs 1.0 / long vs double**: `host-long?` mirrors the design's own
  per-host rule exactly (JVM boxed-integer instance check; CLJS `js/
  Number.isSafeInteger`; CLJD `int?`, the same predicate `yin.vm.debruijn/
  numeric-class` already uses for this host). `host-double?` is the
  portable residual: any number that is none of long/ratio/bigint. This
  makes 1 and 1.0 classify :long/:double distinctly on JVM and Dart, and
  identically on CLJS (both :long, since `1.0` is a safe integer there) --
  exactly the design's stated CLJS limitation, and exactly why the CLJS-
  vs-JVM/Dart split is enforced in the test with `#?(:cljs nil :default
  ...)`, not asserted universally.

- **Chars**: `host-char?` is `#?(:cljs false :default (char? v))`. The
  design's S2 prose says "Dart and JS have no character type," but the
  completion-criteria list explicitly demands a JVM-AND-Dart char-
  distinctness test, which only makes sense if Dart's `char?` is a real,
  unambiguous predicate (unlike CLJS's, which is `(and (string? x) (==
  (.-length x) 1))` -- true for any length-1 string, which would silently
  misclassify an ordinary short string as a char). I resolved this in
  favor of the binding, testable completion-criteria list over the looser
  summary prose: CLJS never returns :char (avoiding the misclassification
  risk entirely, since I could not verify CLJS's `char?` semantics are
  safe to rely on here), JVM and Dart both use `char?` directly. This
  could not be verified on an actual CLJD build (see "what I could not
  run"); if ClojureDart's `char?` turns out to be as ambiguous as CLJS's,
  this decision needs revisiting on that host specifically.

- **Ratios**: supported with their own :ratio tag on JVM and Dart
  (`host-ratio?` = `#?(:cljs false :default (ratio? v))`), refused with
  :unsupported-value on CLJS, which the design states has no ratio type.
  Encoded as two framed :long parts (numerator, denominator). The CLJS
  branch of `encode-scalar`'s :ratio case is host-gated to avoid
  referencing `numerator`/`denominator` at all on CLJS, where shadow-cljs
  correctly flagged them as undeclared vars even though the branch is
  unreachable there (`case` compiles every branch regardless of
  reachability) -- fixed by wrapping that one case arm in `#?(:cljs
  (framed :ratio "") :default ...)`; confirmed 0 shadow-cljs warnings
  afterward.

- **Bigints**: supported only on JVM (`host-bigint?` checks `clojure.lang.
  BigInt`/`BigInteger` specifically, since `int?` is true for both Long
  and BigInt on the JVM and so cannot tell them apart). Refused on both
  CLJS and CLJD. I looked for an existing repo pattern first, per the
  prompt's instruction: `yin.vm.debruijn/numeric-class`'s `:cljd` branch
  is `(if (int? v) :int64 (double-class v))` -- it does not attempt
  bigint detection on Dart either, falling straight to double-class for
  anything `int?` doesn't claim. I matched that absence rather than guess
  an unverified ClojureDart class name (e.g. a `cljd.core/BigInt`-shaped
  reference), which risks either silently misclassifying a value or
  failing to compile outright -- a guess I could not check, since the
  CLJD lane was denied (see below). This is stated as a documented,
  reviewable choice, not a verified fact about ClojureDart's actual
  bigint representation.

`host-supported-scalar-classes` collects these into one set per host via
a single `remove` over `scalar-classes`, keyed by a `#?()` of per-host
exclusion sets, so the completion-criteria's "CLJS excludes :char/:ratio/
:bigint, CLJD excludes :bigint, JVM excludes nothing" claim is one
literal table rather than scattered conditionals.

## No decode/receive path (a deviation, stated explicitly)

S2's CLJS-refuses-on-decode discussion ("the refusal matters on DECODE/
RECEIVE of a foreign image... implement it in the decode/load path, not
encode") describes a receive-and-verify operation this phase's file box
does not include: B1 only builds hand-built-fixture-level encode/hash/
validate, no lowerer (B2) and no loader (B3/B6). I did not write a decode-
from-wire-bytes function, since there is nothing in this file that would
call it and nothing in the completion-criteria list asks for one --
every listed criterion is phrased in terms of a Clojure-level image
(`well-formed-image?`, `image-hash`, `encode-image`/`encode-scalar`), not
raw bytes. `validate-host-support`/`image-scalar-classes` do implement the
*other* half of item 5 (deriving used classes from an already-Clojure-
level image's :const operands and refusing per host), which does not need
a byte parser. This is a scope decision, not an oversight; flagging it so
B2/B3/B6 know the CLJS-decode refusal is still unimplemented anywhere in
the tree.

